{"id": "ed984a4a-1e73-4aa0-9a3a-bbebdca572af", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This EKS compliance check is bounded but spans multiple resource calls, so it's token-inefficient for me to handle directly—better to delegate to a subagent after outlining the plan for the user. I'll let the subagent work out the specifics of accessing the Kubernetes API, likely through EKS access patterns.

For the control checklist, I'm pulling together CIS EKS benchmark items: cluster-admin role usage, wildcard permissions in Roles/ClusterRoles, privileged containers, host namespace sharing (hostPID/hostIPC/hostNetwork), privilege escalation, CNI version/network policy requirements, and default namespace usage. Some of these benchmark numbers are ambiguous across versions, so I'll have the subagent check both interpretations and flag any AccessDenied errors while keeping everything read-only.

"}]}

{"id": "099e8705-4f22-4152-8ac7-ddef487c68e8", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll run a read-only check of cis-skill-test against those six controls. I'll flag any reads that get denied.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_nirkZndZ3RtX6AjVTPxbRo", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "eks-cis-check", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Run a READ-ONLY CIS Amazon EKS Benchmark compliance check on EKS cluster 'cis-skill-test' in us-east-1 (account 012345678901_1, the connected account). Evaluate controls 4.1.1, 4.1.3, 4.2.1, 4.2.4, 4.4.1, 4.5.2 and report for each: status (PASS / FAIL / MANUAL / NOT ASSESSABLE) plus the exact offending resources (kind, namespace, name, and the specific field or rule that violates).",
  "context": "The user wants a per-control status with offending resources, and an explicit list of any read that was denied (which API/call, which resource, and the error message). Never mutate anything. Start with eks describe-cluster / list-access-entries / list-addons etc. via use_aws, and find whatever route is available to read Kubernetes objects (RBAC, pods, namespaces, NetworkPolicies, DaemonSets, etc.), e.g. through the Kubernetes API with the available credentials or any configured kubernetes/EKS tooling. If Kubernetes API reads are not possible, say exactly why and mark controls NOT ASSESSABLE rather than guessing.",
  "controls": {
    "4.1.1": "Ensure the cluster-admin role is only used where required: list ClusterRoleBindings/RoleBindings referencing ClusterRole cluster-admin, and flag subjects other than expected system bindings (e.g. system:masters group or EKS-managed ones are noted but identify any user/group/serviceaccount subjects).",
    "4.1.3": "Minimize wildcard use in Roles and ClusterRoles: find Roles/ClusterRoles with '*' in verbs, resources, or apiGroups (distinguish system:/EKS-managed defaults from user-created ones, but list both).",
    "4.2.1": "Minimize admission of privileged containers: find pods/workloads (containers, initContainers) with securityContext.privileged=true; also note whether Pod Security Admission labels / policy enforcement exist on namespaces.",
    "4.2.4": "Minimize admission of containers wishing to share the host network namespace: find pods with hostNetwork=true (note kube-system/aws-node/kube-proxy as system-managed but still list them).",
    "4.4.1": "CNI: in the CIS EKS benchmark version this is either 'Ensure latest CNI version is used' or 'Ensure CNI plugin supports network policies'. Evaluate both interpretations: report the installed vpc-cni addon version vs latest available for the cluster's Kubernetes version (eks describe-addon / describe-addon-versions), and whether network policy support is enabled (enableNetworkPolicy in addon configuration, or another policy engine). State which interpretation each result belongs to.",
    "4.5.2": "The default namespace should not be used: list any user workloads/resources (pods, deployments, services, configmaps, secrets, etc., excluding the built-in 'kubernetes' service and kube-root-ca.crt configmap) living in namespace 'default'."
  },
  "criteria": "Return a concise table-ready summary: control -> status -> offending resources -> evidence. Then a separate section 'Denied / failed reads' listing every denied or failed call with its error, and which control it impacted. Be factual; do not infer results for reads that failed."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Run CIS EKS benchmark checks on cluster cis-skill-test"}], "created_at": null}

